		"summary": "Open a few terminal windows in a row and they step across the screen again instead of landing on top of one another. A new window is placed before its offset is applied, and windows with a hidden title bar now cascade like every other kind, so the arrangement you expect happens whichever style you use.\n\nNew tabs also arrive cleanly. The brief flash you could catch when opening one is gone, because the background is in place before the tab is drawn. A search holds onto its results when a program writes more output above them, and Find Previous now moves backward when the search field has focus, so both directions take you where you asked to go. Opening a link or a file no longer leaves a background helper spinning on a CPU core.\n\nThe week closes on how your keystrokes travel to the program you're running. Control key combinations, the numeric keypad, F13 through F25, Alt+Escape, and non-ASCII characters typed with Alt now report through the same modern encoding path, so editors and multiplexers that ask for it see exactly the key you pressed. The terminal also starts with less fixed memory, keeping the foundation current without changing how you work.\n\nThe week closes in source control. The bundled Git tool moves up a release, and most of what changed is about not losing your place: stop in the middle of a rebase and the conflicted commit is already selected, resolve a conflict and the file you were on stays selected, and files you have already settled remain in the list instead of disappearing the moment they are fixed. Diffs no longer flicker or glitch while they load, the tool starts faster, and the keybinding and recent-repository menus narrow as you type. Refreshed terminal color themes round out the week.",
