		"summary": "You can now choose exactly which microphone Voice uses. Settings > Sounds > Dictation lists every input, names the device behind System Default, and keeps a disconnected choice visible instead of silently switching. The picker now appears reliably, so the control you read about is finally where you expect it.\n\nYEN also gets lighter when you leave it open. An idle terminal parks its display timer, hidden windows release their graphics memory, and both return immediately when you need them. Clipboard exchanges stay behind clear approval prompts, dropped text behaves like a paste, and stricter validation keeps oversized or malformed clipboard writes out.\n\nThe week closes with input and output behaving more precisely. Control codes, terminal images, physical keys, wrapping, shortcuts, and cursor changes now follow their intended rules more closely. AppleScript key commands translate each key once through your current keyboard layout, while updated emoji font lookup keeps scripted input and expressive output dependable. And when you close a window with several programs still running, YEN now offers to walk you through those windows one at a time, so a long-running job is something you decide about rather than something you lose. That care now extends to the windows you are not watching: when output arrives in a background window, it is drawn at the display's own pace instead of waiting for your attention, so the build you left running stays readable at a glance. Images handed to the terminal through shared memory are checked against the naming rules first, and a malformed request is refused rather than opened. A final engine maintenance pass keeps the foundation current without changing anything you touch.",
